For each assignment, copy this file into your homework
submission directory and answer all questions.

Did you write all the code yourself?
   - Don't copy and paste code from the internet, your
     classmates, sample solutions, or anywhere else.
     Review https://stevens.netmeister.org/631/#plagiarism

Yes. All code was written entirely by our team from scratch specifically
for this project, adhering strictly to RFC 1945, RFC 3875, the sws(1) manual
page, and the Stevens CS631 academic integrity guidelines.


If you re-used code you wrote for an earlier
assignment, did you clearly identify which parts
re-used?

No external or previous assignment code was re-used. All components (networking,
HTTP parsing, directory indexing, CGI engine, and logging) were newly authored.


Does your code follow the style guide?
   - Code is communication.  Make sure your code is
     readable, well-structured, properly indented,
     with sensibily named variables and functions.

Yes. All source files strictly follow the NetBSD Kernel Normal Form (KNF)
style guide as specified in https://stevens.netmeister.org/631/style:
  - 8-space tab indentation.
  - Line breaks kept under 80 characters.
  - Function declarations and definitions on separate lines with prototypes.
  - Variable declarations grouped and sorted by type size and alphabetically,
    with no initialization in declaration statements.
  - No space after casts or sizeof operators.
  - Explicit pointer comparisons against NULL.
  - Clean, meaningful comments written in complete English sentences.


Does your code compile without warnings or errors on a
NetBSD 11.0 system?
  - Always use *at least* 'cc -Wall -Werror -Wextra',
    possibly
    'cc -Wall -Werror -Wextra -Wformat=2 -Wjump-misses-init -Wlogical-op -Wpedantic -Wshadow'.

Yes. The project compiles cleanly without any warnings or errors on
NetBSD 11.0 using the default compiler with:
  CFLAGS = -Wall -Werror -Wextra -Wformat=2 -Wpedantic -Wshadow
In addition, the code compiles and runs portably on Linux (glibc) and OmniOS
(illumos/SunOS).


Did you provide a Makefile?
  - 'make' should use the above CFLAGS and produce an
    executable
  - use suffix rules to compile individual .c files

Yes. The provided Makefile uses suffix rules (.SUFFIXES: .c .o) to compile
individual .c modules, applies strict CFLAGS and CPPFLAGS, links libmagic,
and automatically links -lsocket and -lnsl when built under SunOS/OmniOS.
Default target 'all' builds the 'sws' executable. Targets 'clean' and
'check' are also provided.


Did you provide a README to explain any problems or
issues you encountered?
  - Make sure that your text is well-formatted, plain
    ASCII in proper English.  Use a spell checker.
  - Break your text at around 80 characters.

Yes. A comprehensive README file is provided in plain ASCII, formatted to 80
columns. It covers the server architecture, concurrency model, protocol and CGI
handling, cross-platform portability considerations, group collaboration,
testing methodologies, and time spent.


Did you check the return value of all functions that
could fail?
  - Commonly unchecked functions include: malloc(3),
    gmtime(3), stat(2)

Yes. Every function call that can fail is systematically checked:
  - Memory allocation: malloc(3), realloc(3), strdup(3).
  - Time conversion: gmtime_r(3), strptime(3), timegm(3).
  - File system: stat(2), open(2), opendir(3), readdir(3), access(2),
    realpath(3), getcwd(3).
  - Process & Signals: fork(2), pipe(2), dup2(2), waitpid(2), sigaction(2).
  - Networking: socket(2), setsockopt(2), bind(2), listen(2), poll(2),
    accept(2), getaddrinfo(3), getnameinfo(3).
  - User database: getpwnam(3).


Did you send error messages to stderr?  Did you use
strerror(3) or perror(3) etc. to print meaningful
error messages?

Yes. BSD error routines err(3), errx(3), warn(3), and warnx(3) are utilized
throughout the codebase to output descriptive error diagnostics to stderr.


Did you make sure that your .h files only include function
forward declarations, macros, etc., no function
implementations or other code?

Yes. All .h header files contain only include guards, macro definitions,
struct definitions, and function prototypes. No function implementations or
variable instantiations exist in .h files.


Did you check that your code implements the best
practices we discussed (e.g., avoidance of magic
numbers, code duplication, appropriate use of
subroutines, comments, ...)?

Yes. All magic numbers are defined as macros (e.g., DEFAULT_PORT, MAX_REQ_BUF,
HTTP status codes). Common logic is decomposed into dedicated helper functions.
Clear modular boundaries separate networking, HTTP parsing, filesystem
operations, CGI execution, logging, and portability.


For any string manipulation, did you use size-bounded
functions?
  - Use strlcat(3)/strlcpy(3) instead of
    strcat(3)/strcpy(3) etc.

Yes. All string operations exclusively use size-bounded functions: strlcpy(3),
strlcat(3), and snprintf(3). A portable compatibility implementation of
strlcpy(3) and strlcat(3) is included in compat.c for environments that do
not provide them natively.


How long did it take you roughly to complete the
assigment?

Approximately 40 to 50 hours of combined team effort across design,
implementation, debugging, cross-platform testing, and documentation.
